WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.038
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.038 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Privileged Access.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.101
This is Chapter 1: privileged
inventory as code.

4
00:00:09.101 --> 00:00:14.205
You can't make privilege just-in-time until
you can list it: who holds which role,

5
00:00:14.205 --> 00:00:16.598
at which scope, standing or eligible,

6
00:00:16.598 --> 00:00:19.550
directly or through a
group, and in which tier.

7
00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:23.780
We'll start from Microsoft's own
samples for listing role assignments,

8
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:26.187
see what they miss, then build the reader,

9
00:00:26.187 --> 00:00:30.125
the rows and the findings, and finish
with two labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:30.125 --> 00:00:35.630
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:40.993
Why start with an inventory?

12
00:00:40.993 --> 00:00:43.205
Because you can't fix what you can't list.

13
00:00:43.205 --> 00:00:48.020
A role reaches a person in four ways
that look the same on an admin's screen

14
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:53.155
and very different to an attacker:
permanent or time-bound, eligible or active.

15
00:00:53.155 --> 00:00:55.855
An activated role looks
active for a while,

16
00:00:55.855 --> 00:00:58.555
and a role on a group
reaches everyone in it.

17
00:00:58.555 --> 00:01:03.648
Microsoft's own target is zero permanently
active assignments outside the emergency

18
00:01:03.648 --> 00:01:08.317
accounts, and the program wants roles to
reach people only through named groups.

19
00:01:08.317 --> 00:01:10.423
So the first gate is simply this:

20
00:01:10.423 --> 00:01:14.090
know what's standing today,
and classify it by tier.

21
00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.203
Next, which cloud.

22
00:01:19.203 --> 00:01:25.052
For this estate's tenant, Microsoft 365
GCC with commercial Azure, every host is a

23
00:01:25.052 --> 00:01:31.166
worldwide one: the normal Graph endpoint
and the normal Resource Manager endpoint.

24
00:01:31.166 --> 00:01:35.565
GCC High and the Defense Department
cloud use their own hosts.

25
00:01:35.565 --> 00:01:38.943
The licenses for GCC are
hosted in Azure commercial,

26
00:01:38.943 --> 00:01:43.106
and the governance product that
PIM needs is available in every

27
00:01:43.106 --> 00:01:46.877
one of these clouds, as is every
Graph call the chapter uses.

28
00:01:46.877 --> 00:01:50.472
The code never guesses: you
name the cloud explicitly,

29
00:01:50.472 --> 00:01:53.780
and it refuses any name
it doesn't recognize.

30
00:01:57.530 --> 00:01:59.642
So what does the inventory actually read?

31
00:01:59.642 --> 00:02:04.253
On the Microsoft Entra side: the role
definitions, so every row gets a name;

32
00:02:04.253 --> 00:02:08.786
the active assignments, with whether
each one is assigned or activated,

33
00:02:08.786 --> 00:02:13.089
direct or through a group, and when
it ends; the eligible assignments,

34
00:02:13.089 --> 00:02:17.468
with their expiration; and the
members of each role-assignable group.

35
00:02:17.468 --> 00:02:20.925
On the Azure side, two Resource
Manager collections give

36
00:02:20.925 --> 00:02:24.530
active and eligible role
schedules for each scope you name.

37
00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:28.193
Everything is on the stable,
generally available versions.

38
00:02:28.193 --> 00:02:30.610
Next, where the tier comes from.

39
00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:35.798
Where does the tier come from?

40
00:02:35.798 --> 00:02:37.211
Not from Microsoft.

41
00:02:37.211 --> 00:02:42.296
Microsoft does label some roles as
privileged, but that label is still a preview,

42
00:02:42.296 --> 00:02:44.300
read through the beta endpoint,

43
00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:47.460
so the program treats it as
an input, not the answer.

44
00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.424
The tiers live in a small
file the program owns,

45
00:02:50.424 --> 00:02:55.310
keyed by each role's template identifier,
which is the same in every tenant.

46
00:02:55.310 --> 00:03:00.685
Any role that isn't in the file is reported
as unclassified, never assumed safe.

47
00:03:00.685 --> 00:03:06.160
Azure roles get their tier from Book 04's
placement table, by role and by scope.

48
00:03:06.160 --> 00:03:08.620
Both are labeled design guidance.

49
00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:16.708
Microsoft's starting point is Learn's
page on listing role assignments.

50
00:03:16.708 --> 00:03:21.421
It covers the admin center, PowerShell
and Graph itself, and its all-roles

51
00:03:21.421 --> 00:03:26.683
sample reads every role definition, then
lists each role's assignments one by one.

52
00:03:26.683 --> 00:03:30.380
For Azure, Learn says there's no
single command for both kinds,

53
00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.708
so you list eligible and
active schedules separately.

54
00:03:33.708 --> 00:03:38.695
Those samples only live inside the web
page, so the repository pins the page's

55
00:03:38.695 --> 00:03:43.844
source unmodified, checks its hash in a
test, and watches for upstream changes.

56
00:03:43.844 --> 00:03:48.882
A harness then runs the samples,
exactly as published, against stand-ins.

57
00:03:48.882 --> 00:03:50.440
Nothing signs in.

58
00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:55.952
This is the heart of the chapter.

59
00:03:55.952 --> 00:03:59.235
Run against the fixture tenant,
Learn's sample lists the

60
00:03:59.235 --> 00:04:02.590
active assignments well, so
that part is kept as the model.

61
00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:04.770
But it can't see eligibility at all,

62
00:04:04.770 --> 00:04:09.207
and under PIM eligibility is where
almost every human holder ends up,

63
00:04:09.207 --> 00:04:14.578
so an active-only list shrinks as the project
succeeds and stops being an inventory.

64
00:04:14.578 --> 00:04:18.254
It also makes one call per
role, never asks for every page,

65
00:04:18.254 --> 00:04:23.128
and treats a group as a single holder,
so the people behind it are invisible.

66
00:04:23.128 --> 00:04:25.980
Each of those misses is pinned by a test.

67
00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:32.668
The rest of the gaps are
about trust and reach.

68
00:04:32.668 --> 00:04:36.663
The sample prints raw identifiers
with no names and no tier,

69
00:04:36.663 --> 00:04:39.355
and a reviewer can't act on an identifier.

70
00:04:39.355 --> 00:04:43.330
It signs in without naming a
cloud or limiting its permissions.

71
00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:47.140
Its transitive read uses beta
on a hard-coded worldwide host,

72
00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:49.730
which is wrong for the government clouds.

73
00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:51.612
And it says nothing about Azure,

74
00:04:51.612 --> 00:04:55.592
where Owner and User Access
Administrator are just as privileged.

75
00:04:55.592 --> 00:04:59.786
So the verdict is: keep Learn's page
as the model and the cross-check,

76
00:04:59.786 --> 00:05:02.550
but don't use its loop as the inventory.

77
00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:07.238
Now the code.

78
00:05:07.238 --> 00:05:12.501
It's one PowerShell module, two entry
scripts, the call list and the tier map.

79
00:05:12.501 --> 00:05:16.830
Every call goes through one reader,
built for one task in one cloud,

80
00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:21.962
and the only thing that touches the network
is a small script block you hand it:

81
00:05:21.962 --> 00:05:25.250
a live one for real runs, or
a fake for labs and tests.

82
00:05:25.250 --> 00:05:28.575
The reader enforces four
rules, shown on the slide.

83
00:05:28.575 --> 00:05:32.021
It only makes calls the
task declares, only reads,

84
00:05:32.021 --> 00:05:37.730
only sends its token to the cloud's own
host, and always reads to the last page.

85
00:05:41.480 --> 00:05:46.418
What comes out is one row per assignment,
with everything a reviewer needs on it:

86
00:05:46.418 --> 00:05:50.217
which plane, which role and tier,
which scope, what state it's in,

87
00:05:50.217 --> 00:05:54.168
when it ends, and whether it
arrived directly or through a group.

88
00:05:54.168 --> 00:05:57.780
The subtle part is counting
each assignment exactly once.

89
00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:02.419
Microsoft returns group-delivered
assignments as their own active rows,

90
00:06:02.419 --> 00:06:05.842
but the code expands the group
itself, so it skips those.

91
00:06:05.842 --> 00:06:09.573
And Azure returns the same
schedule from more than one scope,

92
00:06:09.573 --> 00:06:13.080
so the code removes duplicates
and skips inherited rows.

93
00:06:13.080 --> 00:06:14.990
Next, the findings.

94
00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:23.277
Test-PaInventory turns those rows
into the nine findings on the slide.

95
00:06:23.277 --> 00:06:28.050
Two thresholds come straight from Learn:
fewer than five Global Administrators,

96
00:06:28.050 --> 00:06:30.828
and no more than three
owners on a subscription.

97
00:06:30.828 --> 00:06:32.807
Learn also backs the biggest one,

98
00:06:32.807 --> 00:06:37.355
standing privilege in the top tiers
outside a short, owned exceptions list,

99
00:06:37.355 --> 00:06:40.949
and the root-scope finding,
because elevating access hands

100
00:06:40.949 --> 00:06:44.690
someone User Access Administrator
over every subscription.

101
00:06:44.690 --> 00:06:49.735
The rest are the program's own design,
labeled that way: roles assigned straight

102
00:06:49.735 --> 00:06:55.060
to people, badly named groups, unmapped
roles, and eligibility that never ends.

103
00:06:58.810 --> 00:07:04.228
Each run writes the inventory, the findings
and a manifest the reviewer can check:

104
00:07:04.228 --> 00:07:09.160
which task, which cloud, every request
with its time, and a hash of every file.

105
00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:14.923
The manifest names no one, but the inventory
does, so treat the folder as sensitive.

106
00:07:14.923 --> 00:07:17.723
The chapter's tests run
with no tenant at all.

107
00:07:17.723 --> 00:07:21.050
And here's an honest gap:
Learn documents that the stable

108
00:07:21.050 --> 00:07:24.522
members call doesn't return
service principals in a group.

109
00:07:24.522 --> 00:07:27.260
Neither workaround fits
the reader's rules,

110
00:07:27.260 --> 00:07:31.200
so that gap is recorded rather
than quietly papered over.

111
00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:36.012
Who is this for?

112
00:07:36.012 --> 00:07:38.176
Developers build the read path.

113
00:07:38.176 --> 00:07:42.638
Identity engineers and the I S
S O's review what it produces.

114
00:07:42.638 --> 00:07:45.406
The app's permissions are
the union of its calls,

115
00:07:45.406 --> 00:07:49.525
each the least privileged for the job,
and nothing in the list can write.

116
00:07:49.525 --> 00:07:51.427
One choice is worth noticing:

117
00:07:51.427 --> 00:07:56.975
the basic user read permission is added
alongside group members, because without it,

118
00:07:56.975 --> 00:08:01.175
Learn says, a member comes back
with only an identifier and a type.

119
00:08:01.175 --> 00:08:04.790
If a member still comes back
unnamed, the code keeps the row and

120
00:08:04.790 --> 00:08:08.820
raises a warning rather than
dropping someone who holds a role.

121
00:08:12.570 --> 00:08:18.508
Lab 1 needs only a laptop: PowerShell
7 and Pester, no tenant, no network.

122
00:08:18.508 --> 00:08:23.231
First you run Learn's own samples against
the stand-ins, and one test shows what

123
00:08:23.231 --> 00:08:27.882
you'd otherwise take on trust: the
eligible-only holders simply never appear.

124
00:08:27.882 --> 00:08:31.658
Then you list the permissions,
five of them, and none can write.

125
00:08:31.658 --> 00:08:36.694
Finally you run the inventory against a
fictional tenant with three Azure scopes.

126
00:08:36.694 --> 00:08:40.339
It returns thirty rows across
the tiers, ten findings,

127
00:08:40.339 --> 00:08:43.320
seven warnings and one informational item.

128
00:08:43.320 --> 00:08:45.340
Let's read those findings.

129
00:08:49.090 --> 00:08:52.115
Reading the findings is
where the rules become real.

130
00:08:52.115 --> 00:08:56.521
Seven rows are standing privilege in
the top tiers, including a service

131
00:08:56.521 --> 00:09:00.853
principal whose exception nobody
owns, so the exception doesn't count.

132
00:09:00.853 --> 00:09:04.015
One Global Administrator
also elevated to root.

133
00:09:04.015 --> 00:09:08.244
There are five Global Administrators
once the group's members are counted,

134
00:09:08.244 --> 00:09:10.290
and five owners on one subscription.

135
00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:13.133
The emergency account
is only informational,

136
00:09:13.133 --> 00:09:15.902
because it's a listed
exception with an owner.

137
00:09:15.902 --> 00:09:20.341
Then you watch the reader refuse an
undeclared call and an unknown cloud.

138
00:09:20.341 --> 00:09:23.800
The whole suite passes,
three hundred thirty tests.

139
00:09:27.550 --> 00:09:30.808
Lab 2 moves to a test tenant
with fictional people,

140
00:09:30.808 --> 00:09:33.112
never production, and it only reads.

141
00:09:33.112 --> 00:09:37.955
You register the app in the tenant's own
cloud with a certificate, grant exactly

142
00:09:37.955 --> 00:09:42.350
the five permissions plus Reader on a
test subscription, sign in, and run.

143
00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:45.681
Then comes the useful part:
download the admin center's

144
00:09:45.681 --> 00:09:48.863
list of assignments, as
Learn describes, and compare.

145
00:09:48.863 --> 00:09:51.549
Every active row should
be in the inventory,

146
00:09:51.549 --> 00:09:56.175
and the inventory should also hold the
eligible rows the download can't see.

147
00:09:56.175 --> 00:10:00.540
Check the two assumptions the fixture
made, then back everything out.

148
00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:06.528
Every danger here has a guard in the code,

149
00:10:06.528 --> 00:10:09.852
and most of them are about
something quietly going missing.

150
00:10:09.852 --> 00:10:14.140
The worst is an active-only
inventory: as the project succeeds,

151
00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:17.839
the list shrinks, and the gate
passes on an empty table.

152
00:10:17.839 --> 00:10:21.878
Close behind are a group counted
as one person, a member dropped

153
00:10:21.878 --> 00:10:26.227
because its name couldn't be read,
and an exceptions list nobody owns.

154
00:10:26.227 --> 00:10:31.090
Notice the one honest gap: service principals
in a group aren't returned at all.

155
00:10:31.090 --> 00:10:33.943
And remember the inventory
itself is sensitive,

156
00:10:33.943 --> 00:10:36.800
because it names every privileged holder.

157
00:10:40.550 --> 00:10:41.900
Here's what done looks like.

158
00:10:41.900 --> 00:10:45.696
The inventory reads eligible
as well as active assignments,

159
00:10:45.696 --> 00:10:49.113
and its counts match what the
admin center and PIM show.

160
00:10:49.113 --> 00:10:53.373
Every group is expanded with nobody
dropped, every role has a tier

161
00:10:53.373 --> 00:10:57.476
or an owned decision, and the
exceptions list is short and owned.

162
00:10:57.476 --> 00:11:01.832
On the other side, the app holds
exactly its five permissions and Reader

163
00:11:01.832 --> 00:11:06.188
with a certificate, the cloud is
explicit, and the evidence hashes match.

164
00:11:06.188 --> 00:11:09.587
Next, Chapter 2 builds the
emergency access accounts,

165
00:11:09.587 --> 00:11:12.600
the one exception this
inventory lets through.

166
00:11:12.600 --> 00:11:13.760
Thanks for watching.
